WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.591
AI gets stuck on everything now.

00:00:02.611 --> 00:00:06.167
Collars, tractors, the app
that says rain on Thursday.

00:00:06.187 --> 00:00:08.534
So what does it mean?

00:00:08.984 --> 00:00:11.911
Under the label there are
really three kinds of tool.

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:13.908
Once you can tell them apart,

00:00:14.255 --> 00:00:17.693
the sales pitch gets a
lot easier to judge.

00:00:18.143 --> 00:00:20.745
The first kind is AI that senses.

00:00:20.837 --> 00:00:23.973
It takes readings from the
world and spots a pattern.

00:00:24.436 --> 00:00:28.652
A collar notices a cow is
chewing her cud less than usual.

00:00:28.905 --> 00:00:32.471
A camera on a sprayer
tells a weed from a crop.

00:00:32.921 --> 00:00:35.697
The second kind is AI that predicts.

00:00:35.858 --> 00:00:40.318
It learns from years of past numbers
and makes a call about what comes next.

00:00:40.781 --> 00:00:44.254
Pasture growth, the
weather, lambing dates.

00:00:44.415 --> 00:00:47.923
What you get is a best guess
with some spread either side.

00:00:48.084 --> 00:00:50.314
It's never a promise.

00:00:50.764 --> 00:00:53.668
The third kind is AI
that writes and talks.

00:00:53.922 --> 00:01:00.193
ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, Copilot,
Amazon Quick and others.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:05.081
You ask a question in normal words,
and it answers in normal words.

00:01:05.335 --> 00:01:09.471
This is what most people mean by AI
now, and it's what this course spends

00:01:09.491 --> 00:01:11.594
most of its time on.

00:01:12.044 --> 00:01:13.359
Try a quick test.

00:01:13.379 --> 00:01:15.275
A collar that flags a cow on heat?

00:01:15.295 --> 00:01:16.481
That's sensing.

00:01:16.734 --> 00:01:19.952
A forecast that says rain on Thursday?

00:01:19.985 --> 00:01:21.009
Predicting.

00:01:21.262 --> 00:01:24.178
Your phone turning your
voice into a text message?

00:01:24.269 --> 00:01:26.094
Writing and talking.

00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:29.390
So how does a chat AI
come up with an answer?

00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:32.293
It learned from a huge amount of text.

00:01:32.639 --> 00:01:35.799
Websites, books, manuals, forum posts.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:40.919
From all of it, it learned which
words tend to follow which.

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:43.275
Try finishing this sentence.

00:01:43.366 --> 00:01:46.746
After weaning, we moved
the ewes back onto the...

00:01:47.348 --> 00:01:50.056
You probably thought hill, or back block.

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:51.564
Not beach.

00:01:52.014 --> 00:01:55.326
A chat AI does the same thing, very fast.

00:01:55.346 --> 00:01:59.459
It writes one small piece at a time,
and each time it picks a likely next

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:00.909
word.

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:03.532
That one idea explains a lot.

00:02:04.134 --> 00:02:08.466
It has taken in so much that its
likely words are often useful,

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.856
so it writes clear text and
explains things plainly.

00:02:12.203 --> 00:02:14.980
But a likely answer
isn't always a true one.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:20.146
When it doesn't know, it still
writes something that sounds right.

00:02:20.596 --> 00:02:24.163
Some apps can search the web,
or read a file you give them,

00:02:24.183 --> 00:02:25.439
and that helps.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:29.003
They still write the answer
by picking likely words.

00:02:29.164 --> 00:02:31.070
So the habit stays the same.

00:02:31.161 --> 00:02:33.671
Check what matters.

00:02:34.121 --> 00:02:37.722
Below the video, sort some farm
tools into the three kinds.

00:02:37.883 --> 00:02:41.809
Then we'll look at where AI already
turns up on New Zealand farms.
